    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests pass).\n\n## Summary\n\nI added a `timezone` field derived from the owner's locality/region via the fixed region-to-timezone table.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **New util `RegionTimezone`** (`util/RegionTimezone.java`) \u2014 a small, single-responsibility lookup class mirroring the existing `CityRegion`/`PostcodeRange` pattern. It holds the pinned table (`NSW\u2192Australia/Sydney`, `VIC\u2192Australia/Melbourne`, `QLD\u2192Australia/Brisbane`) and exposes `of(region)`, returning `null` for regions with no entry (e.g. `UNKNOWN`), matching the test's `REGION_TIMEZONE.get(...)` semantics.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 added a `timezone(Owner)` default method that reuses the existing `locality(owner)` derivation and delegates to `RegionTimezone.of(...)`, plus the `@Mapping(target = \"timezone\", ...)` and the import. This keeps the region concept single-sourced (timezone derives from the same locality string) rather than re-deriving from postcode/city.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `timezone` schema field so the generated `OwnerDto` includes it (documented as derived from locality via the pinned table, null when the locality has no entry).\n\nThe `coreReturnsTimezoneForRegion` acceptance test (Sydney \u2192 `NSW` \u2192 `Australia/Sydney`) and the entire suite pass.",
